window.DDIA = window.DDIA || {chapters:[], viz:[], system:null};
window.DDIA.chapters.push({
  n: 12, title: "Stream Processing",
  worksheet: "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GAGuVaZvsqp4kuQjCQu5k5UKVsbvKIzoqC3RTtT8m3A/edit",
  why: "Chapter 11 processed a complete, bounded input with nobody waiting. This chapter removes the \"complete\": the input never ends and the output has to be close to current. It's the chapter behind every \"keep the search index fresh,\" \"fan out to followers,\" \"update the cache when the row changes,\" and \"notify the user.\" Two ideas carry it: a log is a durable, replayable, ordered stream, and derived data stays correct when it's built from the change stream of the source of truth instead of by dual-writing. \"Exactly-once\" is the trap: it's a property you construct downstream with idempotence or an atomic commit, never a flag on the broker. The last chapter of the book; after it the lane becomes mock designs. The <a href=\"#/viz/change-stream\">Change Stream Map</a> draws the whole thing.",
  jargon: [
    ["Event", "An immutable record that something happened, with a timestamp. Produced once, consumed by many."],
    ["Stream", "An unbounded sequence of events processed incrementally as they arrive, instead of a bounded file processed all at once."],
    ["Queue-style broker", "SQS, RabbitMQ: a message goes to one consumer, is acked, is deleted. Load-balances tasks and forgets history. Durable, but a second reader gets nothing and replay doesn't exist."],
    ["Log-based broker", "Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis: an append-only, partitioned file on disk. Reading deletes nothing; consumers keep their own offset; retention by time or compaction. A buffer that doesn't forget."],
    ["Topic", "The named log you publish to and subscribe to. Under the name, a set of partitions. Our <code>conversation-events</code> and <code>participant-changes</code>."],
    ["Partition", "One ordered sub-log of a topic, chosen by hashing the key. Total order within; no order across. Same word, same tradeoff as chapter 7; a partition holds millions of keys interleaved because partitions are heavy and keys are many."],
    ["Offset", "A message's position in its partition, assigned at append. Names the message and records where a consumer is. Unrelated across partitions; not stable across a repartition."],
    ["Consumer group", "A set of consumer instances sharing a topic's partitions, each partition read by exactly one member. Parallelism capped by partition count; a rebalance pauses the group."],
    ["Roles, not machines", "Producer writes to the log (our owner, via its relay). Broker stores and serves it. Consumer reads forward from an offset and derives something. Owner is the one we invented: the lease-holding process that decides. One machine can hold several roles."],
    ["Log compaction", "Keep only the latest value per key; delete needs a tombstone kept for a grace period. Bounded by distinct keys. Rebuilds current state per key; destroys history, so never for the message log."],
    ["Tombstone", "A record meaning \"this key was deleted,\" since an append-only structure can't remove in place. Kafka: key with null value. Cassandra: a deletion timestamp. LSM: a delete marker in the newest segment. A system primitive, not an application convention."],
    ["Dual write", "The app writes the database and then the cache/index/queue as two steps. Fails on a crash between them (silent permanent gap) and on a race between two writers (permanent wrong order), with no failure needed."],
    ["Change data capture", "A connector reads the database's own replication log, decodes row changes, and publishes them as an ordered event stream so other systems can follow the database. The write-ahead log is the thing; CDC is the tap. Not replica catch-up; that's chapter 6."],
    ["Replication slot", "How the database tracks a CDC reader's position and keeps its log until that reader advances. One connector, one slot; a connector down for a week fills the primary's disk."],
    ["Outbox", "The app writes the business row and an event row in one database transaction; a relay publishes the event. Kills the dual write; events carry intent. Production shape: CDC on the outbox table."],
    ["Event sourcing", "The application writes events as the source of truth; state is a derived view. History is the product (our message log is one). Can't be compacted; needs snapshots for replay speed and a derived view for reads."],
    ["Event time vs processing time", "When it happened vs when the pipeline saw it. Event time for anything about the world; processing time only for anything about the pipeline. Test: does the answer change if you restart the consumer?"],
    ["Window", "Tumbling (fixed, no overlap), hopping (fixed, overlapping), sliding (every event's trailing interval), session (gap-based)."],
    ["Watermark", "The processor's declaration that no event earlier than T is expected, so windows can close. A guess with one dial: tight means fast and more stragglers; loose means late and fewer. An idle input freezes it."],
    ["The three joins", "Stream–stream within a time window (searches × clicks); stream–table against a local copy fed by the table's changelog (events × participants); table–table as a materialized view that updates when either side changes (the inbox copy)."],
    ["Stream–table duality", "A table is a fold over its changelog (start empty, apply each change in order); a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Each derives from the other; a compacted topic is where they meet."],
    ["Checkpointed state", "Flink/Kafka Streams keep operator state locally and snapshot all state plus input offsets together; recovery is restore, rewind, replay. Exactly-once inside the job for free; slow restarts with big state."],
    ["Idempotent consumer", "Applying the same event twice has the same effect as once. Naturally (set, upsert, delete-by-key) or made so (a seen-before check on event identity, which is the construction of idempotence, not a cheat around it)."],
    ["Version by log position", "Every derived row carries the offset or seq of the last event applied; apply only if newer. Makes replays, retries, and batch backfills commutative. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock."],
    ["Exactly-once (effectively once)", "Delivery is at-least-once; the <i>effect</i> appears once. Three constructions: idempotent effect, atomic commit of output plus offset, or recompute from the log instead of processing deliveries. Not a broker feature."],
    ["Backpressure and lag", "Offset lag (log end minus committed) and time lag (now minus last processed event time). A log absorbs a slow consumer on disk; a queue fills memory; a direct pipe blocks the sender."],
    ["Lambda vs kappa", "Lambda: batch for correctness plus stream for freshness, merged at query time. Kappa: stream only, rebuild by replaying the log through the same code. Either way: online serves, stream patches, batch rebuilds and checks."]
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: "recap", title: "Recap questions", items: [
      { q: "A queue-style broker vs a log-style broker: what does each forget, and when would you pick each?",
        robby: "\"The queue forgets things not yet picked up; the log forgets your place per node.\" Both backwards: the queue forgets after ack; the log stores each group's committed offset.",
        a: "<p><b>Queue forgets the message once acked:</b> delivered to one consumer, deleted. Not messages nobody picked up (they wait, and unacked ones redeliver, breaking order). Memory vs disk is orthogonal; SQS is durable. What it forgets is history. <b>Log forgets nothing until retention or compaction:</b> reading deletes nothing; the broker stores each group's committed offset, a cheap integer the consumer owns and can rewind.</p><p><b>Queue</b> when the unit is a task: expensive per message, stateless interchangeable workers, no order, no replay. <b>Log</b> when it's an event stream: many independent readers at no cost per reader, replay to rebuild, order per key, millions per second. Costs: a slow message stalls its partition and parallelism is capped by partition count.</p>",
        line: "a queue hands out work and forgets it; a log records what happened and lets anyone read it, any time, in order." },
      { q: "Why does a log-based broker scale reads to many consumers with no extra cost per consumer?",
        robby: "\"Because the broker isn't trying to send anything.\" It sends bytes on every pull; the reason is what it keeps per consumer.",
        a: "<p>A queue keeps state per message per consumer (delivered, acked, redeliver, delete), and every ack is a write. A log keeps one integer per consumer group. Every consumer reads the same immutable bytes, cached once in the operating system's page cache, copied file-to-socket without the broker's CPU. Cost per consumer is bandwidth. The exception: a consumer far behind reads old segments from disk and slows the others.</p>",
        line: "one integer per group and the same bytes for everyone; the cost is bandwidth, not bookkeeping." },
      { q: "Partitions and consumer groups: what's guaranteed about ordering, and what isn't?",
        robby: "\"Partitions might overlap, with data missing from one that exists in another.\" Partitions are disjoint; each message lives in exactly one.",
        a: "<p><b>Guaranteed:</b> total order within one partition; per-key order if you choose the key (all of conversation A in one partition); one consumer per partition within a group so that order survives into the consumer. <b>Not:</b> any order across partitions (no answer to \"which came first\"); order after a consumer processes in parallel threads or retries one while moving on; parallelism beyond partition count (extra consumers idle); stability during a rebalance (reassignment replays from the last commit).</p>",
        line: "order is per partition, so put the thing that needs ordering in the key, and accept that parallelism is capped by partition count." },
      { q: "What is log compaction, and what does it let you rebuild?",
        a: "<p>Latest value per key, older values dropped; deletes as tombstones kept for a grace period. Bounded by distinct keys, so a ten-year changelog of a million users is a million records. Rebuilds any current-state-per-key thing: a table, a cache, a stream processor's local copy. Costs history: can rebuild what things are now, never what happened, so the message log is never compacted; the participants changelog can be.</p>",
        line: "latest per key, tombstones for deletes, history gone; the storage for anything that is current-state-per-key." },
      { q: "What's a tombstone, exactly?",
        robby: "\"Is it a predefined set of characters, or up to each developer?\"",
        a: "<p>A system-level primitive, not an application convention, with a consistent idea and per-system encoding: Kafka, a key with a null payload; Cassandra, a cell or row marker with a deletion timestamp that replicas compare and compaction drops after a grace period; LSM engines, a delete marker in the newest segment found before the old value; search indexes, a deleted-docs bitmap per segment. It exists because an append-only structure can't remove in place, so deletion is a new fact that outranks old ones. It becomes the developer's problem only when they build their own append-only thing, where \"deleted\" must be a real event every reader honors.</p>",
        line: "deletion written as a new fact that outranks the old ones; the store defines the encoding." },
      { q: "Why is \"write to the database, then write to the cache\" wrong, and what are the two fixes?",
        robby: "\"Fix it with atomicity across both, or locking, or a dataflow engine; you don't need idempotency.\" Atomicity across two systems is 2PC with a cache as participant; locks don't stop the race; and you do need idempotency.",
        a: "<p><b>Partial failure:</b> database write succeeds, app crashes before the cache write; the cache is stale with no record and nothing repairs it. <b>The race, no failure needed:</b> A writes 1 to the database, B writes 2, B writes 2 to the cache, A writes 1 to the cache. Database says 2, cache says 1, forever.</p><p><b>Fixes, one idea:</b> one writer of truth, everything else derived from its change stream. <b>CDC:</b> app writes only the database; a consumer of its replication log updates the cache in commit order. <b>Outbox:</b> row plus event in one transaction; a relay publishes; the cache consumer applies. Both are at-least-once to the consumer, so the cache write is versioned by log offset, apply-if-newer.</p>",
        line: "a crash leaves a hole and a race leaves the wrong order, both silent; one writer, derive the rest, apply idempotently." },
      { q: "Does the CDC-fed cache mean a copy per node, or one shared cache?",
        robby: "Robby's question, and it produced the rule.",
        a: "<p>Either. <b>Shape A:</b> one shared cache (Redis) updated by a small consumer group; app nodes read it over the network. <b>Shape B:</b> each node is its own consumer group, reads the whole changelog, keeps an in-memory copy; bootstrap by replaying the compacted topic. Trap: fifty nodes as <i>one</i> group split the partitions and each sees a fiftieth.</p><p><b>The rule:</b> local wins when <code>r ≫ W</code>, this node's read rate much larger than the table's total write rate, since every node receives every write regardless of how much it reads. A config table at 10 writes a day and a million reads a second: local. A counter written 10,000 times a second: shared, or no cache at all. And shape B's copies each lag independently, so nodes can disagree; shape A has one copy.</p>",
        line: "local copy when reads per node dwarf total writes; shared cache otherwise; the N cancels out." },
      { q: "Change data capture in one sentence; what's the source of truth and what's derived?",
        robby: "\"CDC derives current state from the write-ahead log to bring a database back after a crash.\" That's the database's own recovery. Said twice; killed in Part 2.",
        a: "<p>CDC reads the database's own replication log and publishes every committed row change as an ordered event stream so other systems can follow the database without the app writing to them. <b>Truth:</b> the database. <b>Derived:</b> everything that consumes the stream: cache, search, warehouse, inbox copy, cross-region copy. Details: events are row images plus log position, not SQL; a new consumer needs a snapshot plus its log position; the database keeps its log until every slot has read it, so the connector is production-critical.</p>",
        line: "the commit log made public; the database is truth and everything downstream is a fold of its changes." },
      { q: "How does CDC's subscription work? Is it broadcast, or do you register addresses?",
        robby: "\"Like radio signals, or registering IPs?\"",
        a: "<p>Neither; everything is pull over ordinary connections, at two places. <b>Hop 1:</b> one connector opens a replication connection to the database, creating a slot; the database tracks that slot's position and keeps log from there. <b>Hop 2:</b> the connector writes each change into a topic, one per table, keyed by primary key. <b>Hop 3:</b> consumers subscribe at the broker by group name, not address, and pull at their own pace. Two hops because the slot is expensive for the database (holds log for its slowest reader), so exactly one reader taps it and the broker is where many cheap readers live.</p>",
        line: "one connector holds the slot, the broker holds the fan-out; nobody registers an address." },
      { q: "Write-ahead log vs CDC: what's the difference?",
        a: "<p>The write-ahead log is the database's private, physical record, written before data pages for crash recovery and replica feeding, truncated once checkpointed. CDC is a process that reads it, decodes row-level changes, and publishes them externally. Owner: database vs connector plus broker. Readers: the database and its replicas vs anyone subscribed. Retention: until checkpointed and every slot has read vs the broker's retention. Purpose: recover and replicate the database vs derive everything else from it.</p>",
        line: "the write-ahead log is the thing; CDC is the tap." },
      { q: "Event sourcing vs CDC: who writes the events, and what do they mean?",
        a: "<p><b>Event sourcing:</b> the application writes the event deliberately as the record of intent (\"Alice added Bob\"); the log is truth, state is derived. <b>CDC:</b> the database produces the change as a side effect (\"row inserted in participants\"); the database is truth, the log is derived. Consequences: event-sourced history can be replayed through new logic for a different view; a CDC log replays only to the table it came from. Event sourcing always comes with a derived current-state view (compactable) and snapshots; the event log itself can't be compacted. The message log <i>is</i> event sourcing: (conv_id, seq) events, edits with ref_seq, the conversation view derived.</p>",
        line: "intent written by the app vs mechanics emitted by the database; one can't be compacted, the other can." },
      { q: "Event time vs processing time: one case where the wrong one changes an answer",
        robby: "\"Using the wrong one changes the order of messages.\" Ordering within a conversation never uses either; the owner assigns seq. Then: \"err toward processing time.\" Backwards.",
        a: "<p><b>Windowed counts.</b> \"Messages per minute\" by processing time: a twenty-minute consumer stall shows a dip then a spike that nobody caused, and a phone reconnecting after two days dumps its queue into the wrong minute. By event time, each message lands in the minute it was sent and a restart of your own consumer changes nothing. Same for daily actives. Processing time is right only for the pipeline: lag, throughput, delivery latency. Clock skew fix: record device send, server receive, and processing time; offset ≈ receive minus send, applied per device.</p>",
        line: "event time for what the world did, processing time for what the pipeline did, and never either for ordering a conversation." },
      { q: "The phone crashed at 12:01 before sending and the message went at 12:04. Which time goes on it?",
        robby: "Robby's concrete case, which exposed \"consumer\" meaning the stream processor, not the phone.",
        a: "<p>Three clocks: device event time 12:01 (tap), server receive 12:04 (entered the system), processing time 12:04:02 or 1:15 if the analytics consumer was down. Store the first two; most products display server receive time (consistent across every participant, independent of the sender's clock); ordering uses seq. Analytics may use either 12:01 or 12:04 as event time, consistently; it must never use the third. The restart test is about the stream processor: windowed by processing time, a consumer down from 12:00 to 1:00 shows zero at noon and a double hour at one; by event time, replay the log and the graph is unchanged. The phone's clock being wrong argues for server receive time as the event time, not for the processor's clock.</p>",
        line: "the phone is the producer; use the server's receive time as event time if device clocks worry you, never the processor's clock." },
      { q: "When would you ever want processing time?",
        a: "<p>Rarely: lag and latency (processing minus event time, and a growing gap is the earliest pipeline alarm); the job's own throughput; timeouts and housekeeping inside the processor (evict idle session windows, flush every five seconds); disposable real-time tickers nobody will compare to last week. Everything else, event time. Its cheapness, no event is ever \"late\" by it, is exactly why it's wrong for anything you'll look at twice.</p>",
        line: "only when the pipeline is the subject." },
      { q: "The four window types and what a watermark does",
        robby: "Windows right; \"I don't know about watermarking.\"",
        a: "<p>Tumbling (fixed, each event in one window), hopping (fixed, overlapping, for smoothing), sliding (every event's trailing interval), session (gap-based, no fixed length). <b>Watermark:</b> the processor's declaration \"no more events earlier than T,\" usually max event time seen minus allowed lateness, taken as the minimum across inputs. Lets a window close. Stragglers after it: drop, emit a correction, or keep state for an allowed-lateness grace. One dial: tight is fast with more stragglers; loose is late with fewer. Classic bug: an idle input freezes the minimum and every window stops closing, with no error.</p>",
        line: "a watermark is a guess that lets windows close; tune the dial, and watch for the idle input that freezes it." },
      { q: "The three joins: one example each",
        robby: "Stream–table: \"query the stream and hand it to the processor.\" Backwards: the event looks up the table, in a local copy. Table–table: \"a classic join, conversations per user.\" That's an aggregate.",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–stream:</b> searches and clicks matched within an hour by session ID; state on both sides; \"no click\" emitted when the window closes. <b>Stream–table:</b> each event enriched from a local copy of the table kept current by the table's changelog, never a remote lookup per event; two inputs, the events and the changelog. <b>Table–table:</b> two changelogs maintained as one materialized view that updates when either side changes: the inbox copy, participants joined to messages. Subtlety: which version of the table? Old events enriched with today's row unless the table is versioned by time.</p>",
        line: "stream–stream in a window, stream–table against a local copy, table–table as a view; and ask which version of the table." },
      { q: "Exactly-once: what it means, and the two ways to get it",
        robby: "\"Idempotency, or locking.\" Locking stops concurrent duplicates; the real duplicate is the same consumer an hour later after a crash.",
        a: "<p>Delivery is at-least-once, always; exactly-once means each event's <i>effect</i> appears once despite redelivery (\"effectively once\"). <b>Way 1, idempotent consumer:</b> set-not-increment when the event carries the value; otherwise version every row by event identity, apply-if-newer. <b>Way 2, atomic commit of output and offset:</b> results and consumer position in one transaction, in the output database or via Kafka transactions. Not locking.</p>",
        line: "at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent write, or output and offset in one commit; the broker can't give it to you alone." }
    ]},
    { id: "tradeoffs", title: "Mechanisms as tradeoffs", items: [
      { q: "A queue-style broker: one delivery, ack, delete",
        robby: "\"Consumers can't rebuild state, so you need redundancy or database replication across nodes.\" Queue consumers are stateless workers; there's no state to restore. A log-world worry in the wrong model.",
        when: "The unit is a task (transcode, email, resize): expensive per message, done once by anyone; stateless interchangeable workers the queue load-balances; order irrelevant, no second reader, no replay.",
        breaks: ["No replay, no second reader: acked is gone; a worker bug can't be fixed by re-running history.", "No fan-out: two readers means two queues fed upstream.", "Ordering dies on redelivery.", "Visibility timeout: a slow worker's task is redelivered to a second worker while the first still runs; every consumer needs an idempotency key.", "Poison messages redeliver forever without a retry limit and a dead-letter queue.", "Throughput ceiling from per-message bookkeeping."],
        line: "a queue for work you do once with stateless workers; the moment a second reader, order, or replay shows up, it's a log." },
      { q: "Could a queue require all N subscribers to ack before deleting, instead of a log?",
        robby: "Robby's design question; the answer is the reason logs won.",
        when: "It exists (durable pub/sub subscriptions). All of N, not a quorum, since each subscriber must do its own work.",
        breaks: ["Per-message, per-subscriber ack state: the bookkeeping that caps queues, times N.", "The slowest subscriber pins everything, the replication-slot trap.", "Subscribers must be registered up front; a new one gets nothing from yesterday.", "A log gives the same semantics with no per-message state: retain for a while, let readers track themselves, answer \"has everyone read it\" from the minimum offset."],
        line: "the log's semantics with the queue's mechanics; Kafka dropped the ack-counting and bought disk instead." },
      { q: "A log-based broker (Kafka)",
        robby: "\"Bad for expensive work because the message is replicated N times across N nodes.\" Right conclusion, wrong reason: it's head-of-line blocking.",
        when: "Event streams: fan-out to many independent readers, per-key order, replay to rebuild derived stores, retention as the record, millions per second.",
        breaks: ["Head-of-line blocking: one slow message stalls everything behind it in its partition; a crashed consumer re-does it before moving on.", "Parallelism capped by partition count; adding workers past it does nothing.", "Repartitioning changes key-to-partition mapping: order breaks across the boundary and per-partition local state is on the wrong node.", "Hot partitions from hot keys.", "Rebalances stop the group.", "No per-message delete: tombstone, retention, or crypto-shred.", "Retention is disk, and a lagging consumer hits it.", "Exactly-once is the consumer's job."],
        line: "perfect for events and terrible for tasks: ordered, replayable, fanned out, and one slow message stalls its partition." },
      { q: "More partitions for throughput",
        when: "More consumer parallelism (one consumer per partition is the ceiling); a saturated partition's broker; and only when the key spreads the load. Provision for two years of growth.",
        breaks: ["Ordering scope shrinks to the key; partition by message ID for spread and per-conversation order is gone.", "Repartitioning breaks the key mapping; over-provision up front.", "Hot keys untouched.", "Per-partition overhead: files, replication streams, leader elections; thousands per broker means slow failovers and rebalances.", "Smaller producer batches, higher latency.", "The watermark is the minimum across more partitions, more chances one is idle."],
        line: "enough for the consumers you'll need in two years, a key that spreads, never so many that a failover takes a minute." },
      { q: "Log compaction instead of a snapshot",
        robby: "\"Snapshot monthly and compact the log between snapshots.\" Compaction between snapshots deletes exactly the intermediate values that let you roll back; the pairing is snapshot plus the raw log since.",
        when: "A changelog that rebuilds current state per key continuously with no snapshot step: a cache, a local table copy, processor state, config. Small forever for a users table.",
        breaks: ["History gone: the bad value overwrote the good one.", "Never fully compacted: a replaying consumer sees several values per key and must apply in order.", "High-cardinality keys never shrink; compacting by message ID is a log with extra CPU.", "Tombstone grace period: a consumer down longer misses the delete.", "Broker CPU and IO to rewrite segments.", "Snapshots instead: consistent cutoff and history, but stale, a full copy, and \"replay since\" needs the uncompacted log."],
        line: "compaction for current-state-per-key; snapshots plus a time-retained log for history and cutoffs; never compact what you might need to rewind." },
      { q: "Change data capture from the replication log",
        robby: "\"To restore a node or bring it up to speed.\" Third time; that's replication. CDC is for things that are not the database.",
        when: "Derived systems to keep current (cache, search, warehouse, inbox copy, another region) fed from one stream in commit order with no dual write; the app can't be changed to write an outbox or too many apps write the table; completeness by construction, including migrations and DBA edits.",
        breaks: ["The slot pins the log: a connector down for a week fills the primary's disk; the connector is production-critical.", "Needs database cooperation: logical decoding, privileges, a connector per engine; some managed databases don't expose it.", "Starting consistently needs a snapshot plus its exact log position.", "Schema changes arrive mid-stream and every consumer must cope.", "Events are mechanics, not meaning: \"row inserted,\" not \"Bob joined.\"", "Transactions arrive as pieces unless consumers honor transaction markers.", "A ten-million-row migration is ten million events at once."],
        line: "CDC turns the commit log into a public feed; the price is that the database now waits for its slowest reader." },
      { q: "The outbox pattern",
        robby: "\"Use the outbox when you have less traffic than CDC.\" Traffic isn't the axis.",
        when: "Events should carry intent (\"Bob joined group 7\" from three table writes) with the app owning the schema; you can't or won't tap the replication log; you want to publish only what the app chose. Mechanism: event row in the same transaction as the data row; a relay publishes. Not an alternative to the queue: it's how the event gets into the queue safely.",
        breaks: ["Every write path must remember to write the event; the migration script that forgot is the bug you find in six months. CDC is complete by construction.", "The relay is at-least-once; consumers need idempotency.", "The relay is a slowest reader too; the outbox table grows without cleanup.", "Polling adds latency and load; the production shape is CDC on the outbox table only.", "One extra write per transaction, which is the only throughput cost."],
        line: "CDC gives every change as mechanics; the outbox gives the changes you chose, as intent, at the cost of remembering to write them." },
      { q: "Outbox vs write-ahead log vs queue: line up the roles",
        robby: "\"Event queue is to CDC as outbox is to write-ahead log.\" Crossed pairs.",
        when: "Three roles in both paths. Where the change is recorded inside the transaction: the write-ahead log (CDC path) or the outbox table (outbox path). The process that reads and publishes: the CDC connector or the relay. The destination: the same queue or log in both. So outbox table is to write-ahead log as relay is to connector, and the queue is what they share.",
        breaks: ["The only difference is the first row: the write-ahead log is mechanical and written by the database; the outbox is chosen and written by the app.", "Which is why \"CDC on the outbox table\" is the production combination: intent from the app, completeness from the log tap."],
        line: "the queue is downstream of both; what differs is whether the database or the app wrote the thing being tapped." },
      { q: "Event sourcing as the source of truth",
        robby: "\"Human-readable, for audit; but you can't rebuild state from it, so you'd need a write-ahead log.\" Rebuilding state is the entire point; the event log is the write-ahead log, raised to the application.",
        when: "History is the product (the message log); audit and compliance; many views from the same facts with a new view a replay away; debuggability (replay to the moment before the bug; undo as a compensating event).",
        breaks: ["Reads need a derived view, and it lags; read-your-writes must be engineered.", "Validating a command needs current state, and two commands racing on a stale view both pass; optimistic concurrency on the stream version (the owner's seq).", "Event schemas are forever: a 2019 event must parse in 2030; upcasters.", "The log grows without bound; snapshots bound replay time, not storage.", "Deletion vs immutability: crypto-shred.", "A modeling commitment; on a CRUD system it degrades into \"RowUpdated\" events, which is CDC with extra steps."],
        line: "event sourcing when the history is the product or the audit; otherwise you've built a slower database with a better changelog." },
      { q: "A materialized view kept by a stream processor vs computing at read time",
        when: "Reads frequent and the query expensive (a join or aggregate over many rows per request); the read path must not touch the primary; a latency target the live query can't meet. The ratio: view costs events × fan-out writes forever; live costs reads × query cost.",
        breaks: ["Lag: the view is as current as its consumer; read-your-writes on top.", "Drift: no memory of being correct; periodic rebuild and diff.", "Write amplification: one message in a 500-member group is 500 view updates.", "State must survive: checkpoints or a changelog topic, or a crash is a full rebuild.", "Logic changes mean a full replay.", "One view per query shape; a new question is a new view."],
        line: "the view wins when reads are frequent and the query is heavy; a cheap query runs live, a rare heavy one isn't worth maintaining." },
      { q: "Stream–table join with a local copy vs a remote lookup per event",
        when: "Enriching events with data the stream doesn't carry, at high event rates, when the table is write-cold: the r ≫ W rule applied. Co-partition the table's changelog with the stream so each instance holds only its slice.",
        breaks: ["The table doesn't fit: co-partition it.", "Bootstrap: a new instance replays the compacted changelog before processing, stalling its partitions.", "Two inputs, two lags: an event can arrive before the row it references; buffer, retry, or accept a miss.", "Which version? The copy is the table now; historical joins need versioning by time.", "State to protect: rebuilt from the compacted, retained changelog.", "Remote lookup instead: a round trip per event, the database serving the pipeline's load, and replay impossible because it looks up today's table."],
        line: "keep a local copy fed by the changelog, co-partitioned with the stream; query remotely only when the table is write-hot and events are rare." },
      { q: "Windowing by event time with watermarks vs by processing time",
        robby: "\"Processing time for analytics, event time for the events themselves.\" Backwards, the second time that day. Fixed with the restart test.",
        when: "<b>Event time</b> for any number about what users did; must be stable across a replay. <b>Processing time</b> for the pipeline, plus a disposable real-time ticker nobody will compare to last week.",
        breaks: ["Event time: windows need a watermark, which is a guess; device clocks lie (fix with server receive minus device send per device, but a two-days-offline phone is still a straggler); an idle input freezes the watermark; downstream must tolerate revisions.", "Processing time: an outage shows a dip and the catch-up a spike, both fiction; replay is impossible; offline clients land on the day they reconnected."],
        line: "event time for what the world did, processing time for what the pipeline did; if restarting the consumer changes the answer, you used the wrong one." },
      { q: "Checkpointed operator state (Flink style) vs stateless workers with an external store",
        robby: "\"Not familiar with this one.\"",
        when: "<b>Checkpointed:</b> high-throughput windowed aggregates and joins with lots of per-key state; state local, no network per event; state and offsets snapshotted together give exactly-once inside the job for free. <b>External store:</b> other services must read the state; workers must scale freely; heavy per-event work with small state; the team won't run a stateful cluster. Honest split: Flink computes with local state, the result lands in Cassandra where readers are.",
        breaks: ["Checkpointed: checkpoint and recovery time proportional to state; rescaling redistributes key-partitioned state; state is locked inside the job; you operate a stateful cluster.", "External: a round trip per event; the store is the bottleneck and carries the pipeline's load; state and offset not committed together, so exactly-once is back on you; two workers on one key race unless partitioned by key, which rebuilds option A's partitioning without its locality."],
        line: "checkpointed state for the computation, an external store for the result." },
      { q: "At-least-once plus an idempotent consumer vs transactional exactly-once",
        robby: "\"Not all data allows idempotency; you need the max value.\" You need the event's identity stored with the row, not the final value. And: \"is the event ID a cheat code around idempotence?\"",
        when: "<b>Idempotent</b> is the universal default into any store: naturally (set, upsert, delete-by-key) or made so (a seen-before check on event identity: \"if N not applied, add 1 and record N\" is exactly as idempotent as \"set x = 5,\" over a bigger state). <b>Transactional</b> for the interior of a pipeline, Kafka to Kafka: appends have nowhere to put a version, multi-output atomicity (three records to two topics, all or none), and it's a config flag that removes every line of versioning code. Built into Kafka since 2017: a coordinator writes commit markers, producer epochs fence zombies, Kafka Streams and Flink wrap it in one setting.",
        breaks: ["Idempotent: a version column or dedupe table per output; per row, so a three-row effect can crash halfway; side effects can't be versioned (a dedupe row before the send, and a crash between them loses one); needs a stable event identity.", "Transactional: only where output and offset share a system; latency and throughput of a commit per batch; still at-least-once the moment output leaves Kafka for Cassandra, an API, or an email.", "The third construction: don't process deliveries, recompute the derived store from the log (microbatching with batch-ID-named output files is this at batch granularity)."],
        line: "transactional where output and offset share a system; idempotent everywhere else; a real pipeline is transactions inside and idempotency at the sinks." },
      { q: "Replaying the log to rebuild a derived store vs patching it in place",
        robby: "\"Rebuild if you touch every row; patch a few rows; if you're missing a stretch, rewind to the last offset and replay forward.\" Right, with one nit: from the log, not from another store. Then: \"what does drift look like?\"",
        when: "<b>Replay</b> when the wrong rows can't be enumerated (sporadic drift from delivery: duplicates, drops, out-of-order) or every row changes (schema, logic). <b>Patch</b> when the wrong rows can be enumerated: systematic drift from a code bug in a known offset window, or a structural backfill of one column. <b>Partial replay</b> for a consumer that fell behind: rewind to the offset and run forward.",
        breaks: ["Replay: retention must reach the point (else snapshot plus log since); time proportional to history; the consumer must be deterministic; double capacity during the rebuild; cold caches at the flip.", "Patch: no proof (new code on data you know is wrong); drift you didn't look for stays; logic changes don't patch."],
        line: "patch when you can enumerate the wrong rows; otherwise replay, and reconcile anyway for the drift you can't see." }
    ]},
    { id: "mock", title: "Streams in the messaging system", items: [
      { q: "What is the messaging system's change stream? Where does it originate, who publishes it, and what's its partition key?",
        robby: "\"Product events are for analytics; read them from the write-ahead log into a separate analytics database.\" Analytics is one of five consumers, and the messages' store is Cassandra, where a write-ahead-log tap gives per-node, unordered changes.",
        a: "<p><b>Origin: the owner tier.</b> The owner already has every message in total order, deduplicated by the client's random ID, fenced by its epoch, acked by the Cassandra quorum. The stream's hardest properties are things it already established. <b>Publisher:</b> the owner, via an outbox row written in the same Cassandra batch as the message (same partition key, atomic), and a relay that publishes to the topic. For Postgres (participants, conversations): CDC via logical decoding, one connector. <b>Key: conv_id</b>, the owner's shard key, so per-conversation order on the topic is seq order and one consumer instance handles a conversation at a time; consumers needing user_id re-partition downstream. Every event carries (conv_id, seq) as a free idempotency key.</p><p>Consumers: inbox copy, search, notifications, cross-region copy, analytics. Never a second slot on the primary for analytics; one connector, and analytics subscribes like everyone else.</p>",
        line: "the owner already serializes each conversation, so it publishes; the topic is keyed by conversation; everything else is a consumer group." },
      { q: "Owner vs producer vs broker vs consumer: are these roles stream-specific, Kafka-specific, or general?",
        robby: "Robby's thread, and the best one of the chapter: \"isn't the owner just a specialized producer? why can't owners serve consumers directly? so producers only produce analytics? the broker is a buffer that handles each consumer's quirks?\"",
        a: "<p>Roles, not machines; general, with Kafka words for some. Every owner is a producer; not every producer is an owner (the owner decides in the write path, publishing is a side duty). Serve consumers from the owner and it must buffer per consumer, retain for the slowest, survive its own death, fan out to sixty connections, and absorb backpressure on the send path: it has become a durable, replicated, retained, offset-tracking log server, which is a broker, as its second job next to the one that must be fast. Producers write the primary truth; consumers derive <i>product</i> (inbox, search, push, region copy) and analytics last. The broker is a buffer that doesn't forget: rate decoupling plus durability and replication, which is the whole product.</p>",
        line: "the owner decides; the broker remembers; the consumer derives; collapse any two and the survivor has to do both jobs." },
      { q: "Why does a partition interleave many conversations if we partition by conversation ID?",
        a: "<p>It is partitioned by conversation, and conversations are colocated: hash(conv_id) mod 64 puts all of A in one partition, in order. But 64 partitions (a partition is files plus a replication stream plus a leader) against billions of conversations means each partition holds tens of millions, interleaved by arrival. Chapter 7 again: shard key vs shard count. What the key buys is everything about A in one partition in seq order read by one instance; what it can't buy is a per-conversation position, which is why seq exists beside the offset.</p>",
        line: "the key colocates; the count interleaves; one partition per conversation isn't a thing any broker offers." },
      { q: "The inbox copy kept fresh by a stream: design the consumer. Which join, where does state live, what happens on a crash mid-batch?",
        robby: "\"Stream–table; state in memory; a crash loses the queues, so snapshot inboxes every 7 days and replay from there.\" Join right; the output isn't consumer state; seven days of replay for a crash is the corruption recovery, not the crash recovery.",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–table:</b> event (conv_id) looked up against a local participants copy fed by the Postgres CDC topic, co-partitioned by conv_id so each instance holds only its conversations' memberships; fan out to one row per member. <b>State:</b> two kinds, neither only in memory: the participants copy, rebuilt from the compacted topic in minutes; the inbox rows, which are output in Cassandra keyed by user_id and versioned by (conv_id, seq). <b>Crash mid-batch:</b> restart from the committed offset, re-read the two events since, and apply-if-newer absorbs them. The \"crash the inbox consumer\" fault in the map: processed resets to committed, two duplicates skipped, row count unchanged. Why a local copy and not a Postgres lookup per event: 60k lookups a second from the inbox consumer alone, replay enriching old messages with today's membership, Postgres on the critical path.</p>",
        line: "stream–table against a co-partitioned participants copy, output versioned by (conv_id, seq), restart from the committed offset and let apply-if-newer absorb the replay." },
      { q: "Push notifications: one per message, never two, never zero. Walk the guarantee end to end.",
        robby: "\"Attach an event ID to the row.\" Right key; the send is a side effect with no row, so the dedupe record and the send are two steps and their order decides which failure you get.",
        a: "<p>Owner publishes (at-least-once from here). Notify consumer fans out per recipient: (user, conv, seq). <b>Write the dedupe row first</b> (pending; skip if it exists). Send with a collapse key equal to the message identity. Mark sent. Commit the offset. Crash points: before the row, replay proceeds (never zero); between row and send, zero unless a sweeper resends rows pending past a timeout; between send and mark, two unless the provider's collapse key replaces the first on the device; after mark, replay skips (never two). Honest guarantee: at-least-once to the provider, exactly-once on the screen. The key is deterministic (conv_id:seq, never random, or no retry would ever match), checked by our dedupe row before the send and by the provider's queue and the phone's OS after. Skip the send if the recipient's watermark already passed seq.</p>",
        line: "dedupe row before the send, deterministic collapse key after; idempotency at the row plus idempotency at the device, not anything the broker did." },
      { q: "The search index is now incremental. Which join, what latency, how do you reconcile against the chapter 11 rebuild?",
        robby: "\"Stream–stream, joining searches with messages.\" Searches are online reads of the index, not a stream. Then: \"what does compaction look like for search?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Stream–table</b>, same as the inbox, with a tokenizer: event × participants copy → postings per user. <b>Latency: seconds</b>: tokenize, write a small new segment, visible after the refresh interval. What the incremental path accumulates: tiny segments (every query consults all), tombstones until a merge, drift. <b>Reconcile:</b> the nightly batch rebuilds from the snapshot at offset X into a fresh, compacted, tombstone-free set; a consumer replays from X onto it; flip the manifest; diff batch output against the live index for postings with version ≤ X and alert on mismatches. The batch does compaction, tombstone cleanup, and proof; the stream does now.</p><p>Segment merge for search: five messages with \"thursday\" arrived in five segments, each with a one-posting entry; the merge writes one segment with one entry and a five-posting list, dropping tombstoned IDs. Multi-word queries intersect posting lists (not union) with a position check. Different from log compaction, which keeps the latest per key; a search merge keeps all postings.</p>",
        line: "stream–table plus a tokenizer, seconds to visible, nightly rebuild to compact and prove; a search merge keeps every posting, log compaction keeps the latest." },
      { q: "Read receipts and typing indicators: which belong on the durable log?",
        robby: "\"Neither needs to, but if I had to pick, read receipts.\"",
        a: "<p>Test: does derived state depend on it, and is anyone wrong if it's lost? <b>Read receipts: on the log.</b> The unread count is \"seq above the watermark\"; the sender's \"seen\" is the other side's watermark; a replayed inbox has nothing else to rebuild it from. Cheap: one event per (user, conv) advance, merged with max, compactable, coalesced so a scroll is one event. <b>Typing: never.</b> Worth something for three seconds, nothing derived, high-rate, corrected by the next one; in-memory pub/sub or straight through the connection fan-out. Same test sorts presence (ephemeral), mute settings (durable), delivered-to-device (on the log; the double-tick depends on it).</p>",
        line: "on the log if something derived depends on it; read receipts yes, typing never." },
      { q: "Unread count per conversation: the window, the event time, and the phone offline for two days",
        robby: "\"Window is time since the last open; event time is confusing because it's a lack of events.\" Right instinct, resolved by: no time window at all.",
        a: "<p>Unread is \"messages with seq above the watermark,\" or max_seq − watermark: a seq range, not a time range (a three-day-old unread message must still count). Event time is seq, the one clock the conversation has. The offline phone: its \"read up to 40\" arrives two days late while the laptop already advanced to 55; merge with max keeps 55, and the badge never jumps backward. Its local badge is stale; on reconnect the server's view wins. Compaction loses nothing: latest watermark per key is exactly the state. How the max is applied without a race: through the owner, which serializes the conversation, or a conditional write (apply only if greater); never a plain overwrite. One value per (user, conv), devices only propose; per-device rows exist only for delivered-to-device.</p>",
        line: "unread is max_seq minus watermark in log position; the watermark merges with max, so late reads can only lower the count." },
      { q: "A consumer group falls three hours behind on the inbox topic. What do users see, how do you detect it, what are the recovery options?",
        robby: "\"Detect by previews mismatching the latest message.\" A symptom; the metric is lag.",
        a: "<p><b>Users see:</b> stale inbox for every user whose conversations are in the lagging partitions: old previews, wrong order, missing badges. Opening a conversation works, because that view reads Cassandra directly: a lagging derived view degrades, it doesn't lose data. <b>Detect:</b> offset lag and time lag per partition, alerted; plus a freshness probe (synthetic message timed until it appears). <b>Recover:</b> (1) catch up: scale instances to the partition count, then make each faster, then fix the cause (slow store, poison event dead-lettered, a deploy); (2) skip and backfill: reset offsets to the log end so users get current inboxes now, fill the three-hour hole with the chapter 11 batch over that offset range; version-by-offset makes the overlap safe. Prefer (2) when the gap is large, because catching up keeps users stale the whole time.</p><p>Why reading Cassandra per conversation open scales: partition key conv_id, clustering by seq, LIMIT 50, one partition, one node, a few milliseconds; the inbox was the scatter query, which is why it became a derived copy. Hot groups: the owner's in-memory tail and a cache in front.</p>",
        line: "the inbox degrades and the conversation view still works; watch lag, not previews; catch up if you can, skip and backfill if you can't." },
      { q: "Is the Kafka partition offset redundant with seq?",
        robby: "\"Offset is for the system (lag, replay); seq is for the application.\" Right.",
        a: "<p>Both, different coordinates. Offset: where a consumer is, how far behind, where to resume; per partition, which interleaves many conversations; not stable across a repartition. seq: order inside the conversation, unread arithmetic, the idempotency version on derived rows, gap detection (A#5 then A#7 means something was lost before the log; offsets have no gaps by construction). Neither derives from the other.</p>",
        line: "offset says where you are in the log; seq says where you are in the conversation; the log owns one and the app owns the other." },
      { q: "Dual-write audit: every place the app writes two stores, and which fix each gets",
        robby: "\"Idempotent replay I wouldn't use for any; it's for same-store restores from a snapshot.\" It's mandatory in every row; it's what every consumer does on apply.",
        a: "<p>The rule, three questions per row: which single store does the app write, what carries it to the log (outbox if the owner wrote it, CDC if Postgres wrote it), and is every downstream apply idempotent?</p><table><tr><th>Write</th><th>Single write + carrier</th><th>Consumers</th></tr><tr><td>Send</td><td>message + outbox in one Cassandra batch (same partition); relay</td><td>inbox, search, push, region 2, analytics</td></tr><tr><td>Edit / reaction / delete</td><td>same: events with ref_seq through the owner</td><td>inbox preview, search tombstone</td></tr><tr><td>Watermark</td><td>through the owner (applies the max), outbox; not Cassandra CDC (per node, unordered)</td><td>unread view, sender's seen</td></tr><tr><td>Participants</td><td>Postgres; outbox for intent, with CDC on the outbox table</td><td>every owner's list, inbox and search copies, the new member's inbox row</td></tr><tr><td>Profile change</td><td>Postgres; CDC</td><td>previews, search display, caches</td></tr><tr><td>Device tokens</td><td>Postgres; CDC</td><td>notify lookup copy</td></tr><tr><td>Index-mode flag</td><td>Postgres; CDC</td><td>indexer, query path (read both until migrated)</td></tr></table><p>Participant changes are on their own topic, so a removal never queues behind messages; the two topics lag independently, and the membership event carries the seq at which it took effect.</p>",
        line: "one write per path, an outbox if the owner wrote it and CDC if Postgres did, and idempotent apply on every reader with no exceptions." },
      { q: "\"Messages per minute, live, per region\" on a dashboard: window, time, where the number lives",
        robby: "\"Sliding window, event time, an in-memory counter reset every minute, region via UTC offsets, source it from CDC on Cassandra's WAL.\" Event time right; the counter is a processing-time window; region is a field; the log already exists.",
        a: "<p><b>Tumbling one-minute windows</b> (hopping if a smoother line is wanted), by event time, 30-second watermark, corrections allowed. Region is a field on the event: the conversation's home region, not a timezone. <b>Source: a metrics consumer group on the conversation-events topic</b>, like the other five; never Cassandra, never its write-ahead log. State checkpointed; output one row per (region, minute) into a real-time OLAP store or a Redis hash of recent hours; the dashboard reads that. The nightly batch recomputes the same counts from the lake and diffs them, which is the answer when yesterday's dashboard and the report disagree.</p>",
        line: "tumbling by event time, region as a field, a consumer of the topic, landed in a store built for the dashboard." }
    ]},
    { id: "followups", title: "Interviewer follow-ups", items: [
      { q: "\"Why Kafka and not SQS here?\"",
        robby: "\"You'll lose data on SQS.\" SQS is durable; it forgets after consumption. The argument is shape, not reliability.",
        a: "<p>Fan-out: five groups need every message; SQS delivers to one and deletes. Ordering: standard SQS has none; FIFO orders per group at a few thousand a second against our 60k. Replay: rebuild the inbox, re-index, fix a consumer bug by re-running history. Retention as the record for derived stores. And the other half: SQS is right for the work queues in the same system (transcode, email, deletion tasks).</p>",
        line: "five readers, per-conversation order, and rebuild from history; SQS gives none, and we use it for the tasks anyway." },
      { q: "\"What happens when the consumer dies after writing the output but before committing its offset?\"",
        robby: "\"Manually find the last row, compare to the offset, replay to catch up.\" Nothing is manual; it happens on every deploy.",
        a: "<p>It restarts from the committed offset and re-reads the events it already applied. Idempotent apply: rows already carry those (conv_id, seq) versions, the conditional writes fail, nothing moves, duplicates-skipped goes up by two. Transactional: the uncommitted output was never visible; the retry writes and commits with the offset. Manual reconciliation is only for a consumer built with neither. The right tone is boredom: the normal path.</p><p>How the replay stays a no-op: write sets not increments where the event carries the value (preview row, posting with document ID = event ID, unread as a value computed from seq), and a version on the row with a conditional write everywhere else (apply only if new.seq > row.version, which also rejects out-of-order).</p>",
        line: "it replays from the committed offset and the version check makes the replay a no-op; that's the normal path." },
      { q: "\"Explain exactly-once to a product manager in three sentences.\"",
        robby: "Correct for an engineer: idempotent, versioning, seven sentences.",
        a: "<p>If anything in the system fails and retries, the user still sees each message, badge, and notification exactly once. We do that by giving every event a name and refusing to apply the same name twice. So a failure can make something a few seconds late, but it can never make it show up twice or disappear.</p><p>If pushed: under the hood things can be delivered twice; what we guarantee is that the second time has no effect.</p>",
        line: "name the promise, name the mechanism in plain words, name the one thing that can still happen: delay." },
      { q: "\"How do you re-process the last six months of events after fixing a bug in the consumer?\"",
        robby: "Replay to a new consumer, canary, swap, roll back. Right shape. \"Is a replay a stream and a lake rebuild a batch?\" Yes.",
        a: "<p>Retention decides whether replay is possible (six months on the log, tiered to object storage, or a snapshot plus log since; otherwise the lake). The participants topic is compacted, so a replay sees today's membership, not the membership as of each message. <b>The arithmetic:</b> six months at 60k/s is about a trillion events; a stream replay at ten times live speed is weeks. So: a few keys, replay just their partitions; one column across every row, the batch job over the lake (parallel across a thousand machines, overnight), then the stream consumer resumes from the batch's cutoff offset; full stream replay only when the store is small or the window short. Replay reuses the consumer code at a multiple of live speed; batch is bounded by cluster size.</p>",
        line: "replay for short windows or few keys, the lake for long windows or every row, and the stream resumes from the rebuild's offset." },
      { q: "\"We'll just have the app publish an event after it writes the row. What's wrong with that?\"",
        a: "<p>A crash between the two leaves a committed row no consumer will ever hear about, silently and permanently. And with no crash at all, two servers can commit in one order and publish in the other, so the log's order disagrees with the database's and every consumer applies the wrong order. \"Do it atomically\" across database and broker is two-phase commit on the hottest path. The atomicity you want is inside the database: outbox in the same transaction, or CDC where the log's order is the commit order.</p>",
        line: "a crash leaves a hole and a race leaves the wrong order, both silent; put the event in the transaction or read it out of the commit log." },
      { q: "\"How many partitions, and what if you need more later?\"",
        a: "<p>Three constraints, take the max: throughput per partition (tens of MB/s; our 30 MB/s needs two, not binding); consumer parallelism (search at ~5k/s per instance needs 12, the binding one); two years of headroom (64). Say which constraint bound. Repartitioning is a migration: hash mod 64 to mod 128 moves conversation A to a different partition, order breaks across the boundary, every consumer's key-partitioned local state is on the wrong instance; the safe path is a new topic, dual-publish, migrate groups one at a time, retire the old. Too many is its own cost: slow failover, small batches.</p>",
        line: "sized by the slowest consumer's parallelism with two years of headroom; repartitioning is a migration, so over-provision." },
      { q: "\"What's the difference between a Kafka topic and a database table?\"",
        robby: "\"I don't know what a topic is.\" Then: \"what's a fold?\"",
        a: "<p>A topic is the named log you publish to and subscribe to, a set of partitions. Table: current state, one row per key, mutated in place, queryable by anything, identity by primary key, lives until deleted, answers \"what is it now.\" Topic: history, one record per event in order, append only, read sequentially from an offset, identity by partition plus offset, lives until retention, answers \"what happened in what order.\" <b>The duality:</b> a table is a fold over its changelog (start empty, apply each change in order; the result at any point is the table as of that point), and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Each derives from the other; a compacted topic is a table stored as a log.</p>",
        line: "a table is a fold over its changelog and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table; the log is the truth and every table is a fold of a prefix of it." },
      { q: "\"Your dashboard count is wrong for yesterday. Stream bug or batch bug, and how do you find out?\"",
        robby: "\"A stream bug means we're dropping messages in production.\" A metrics consumer bug drops counts, never messages.",
        a: "<p>Both pipelines computed the same number from the same log. <b>Compute it a third way, directly from the log</b> (count events with event time in yesterday's range) and see which pipeline matches. Batch = log, stream differs: stragglers past the watermark, double-counting on restart without idempotent state, a processing-time midnight, lost window state. Stream = log, batch differs: a missed lake partition, a smeared snapshot cutoff, the wrong timezone, a double-applied filter. Both differ the same way: the source (relay gap, event-time stamping, the lake-landing consumer). All three agree: the dashboard's query or cache. Suspect the stream first; the batch exists to check it, unless the lake itself was landed by a stream consumer.</p>",
        line: "count it a third way from the log, see which pipeline matches, and suspect the stream first because the batch exists to check it." }
    ]}
  ],
  takeaways: [
    "A log is a buffer that doesn't forget: durable, replicated, ordered per partition, read by anyone at their own offset. A queue hands out work and forgets it. Events go on a log; tasks go in a queue; a messaging system has both.",
    "One writer of truth, everything else derived from its change stream. The dual write fails on a crash (a silent hole) and on a race (a silent wrong order); the outbox or CDC removes both by making the event part of the one write.",
    "If the owner wrote it, it's an outbox; if Postgres wrote it, it's CDC; and the production shape is CDC on the outbox table. Never tap Cassandra's write-ahead log for a stream the owner already publishes.",
    "The owner decides, the broker remembers, the consumer derives. Every owner is a producer, not every producer is an owner, and collapsing any two roles makes the survivor do both jobs.",
    "Exactly-once is constructed, never delivered: an idempotent effect (naturally, or by a seen-before check on event identity, which is the construction of idempotence), or output and offset in one commit, or recompute from the log. Transactions inside a Kafka-to-Kafka pipeline, idempotency at every sink.",
    "Version every derived row by log position, (conv_id, seq) here, and apply only if newer. It makes replays, retries, batch backfills, and out-of-order events all harmless, and it's the same rule that stitched batch to stream in chapter 11.",
    "Event time for what the world did, processing time for what the pipeline did. If restarting the consumer changes the answer, you used the wrong one. A watermark is a guess with one dial, and an idle input freezes it.",
    "A stream-maintained view is current and can be wrong forever; batch output is stale and heals. Online serves it, the stream patches it, the batch rebuilds it and diffs the stream's work. Patch when you can enumerate the wrong rows; replay otherwise; reconcile anyway.",
    "A table is a fold over its changelog and a changelog is the stream of changes to a table. Compaction is where they meet. The stream–table join is a fold kept locally, co-partitioned with the stream, and it wins when reads per node dwarf total writes.",
    "Two coordinates, never one: the offset says where you are in the log and seq says where you are in the conversation. Partition count is sized by the slowest consumer's parallelism with two years of headroom, because repartitioning is a migration."
  ]
});
